            "Use a hypothetical example with a compressible clothing group and a rigid shoe box. The shoe box may set a larger outer dimension for the combined parcel. A smaller split clothing parcel could behave differently in a weight-based comparison, while the second parcel adds its own starting costs. Calculate both rather than deciding from item count. Keep protection and services comparable so the lower total does not secretly represent a less suitable packing or coverage choice."
